\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
GUI agents emit click coordinates. We generate the English sentence a person would need
instead, fine-tuning Qwen2.5-VL-3B on AndroidControl with no new annotation. A sentence is
scored by \emph{executability}: a separate grounding model, which never sees the gold
coordinate, reads the sentence, and the step counts as correct when it lands within the
conventional tolerance of the element the user touched \emph{and} closer to it than to any
other element, and the action class agrees. Human-written references score $75.7\%$ on
$4{,}463$ touch steps, a contentless sentence $12.0\%$, and a fluent sentence written for a
different screen $6.1\%$. Fine-tuning lifts the model from $47.6\%$ to $59.4\%$ averaged
over two seeds, and the gain survives a second grounder that was not trained on
AndroidControl. We then ask whether naming the element before writing the same sentence
helps. On the one seed we ran of the two we registered, the
descriptor branch scores $57.2\%$, $2.19$ points below the two-seed control mean. That
average hides a sharp split. Splitting that seed's steps post-hoc by the branch's own
descriptor, it beats the control by $5.7$ points on the $1{,}871$ where the descriptor gets
both the name and the point right, and loses $13.1$ on the other $1{,}374$. On this seed the
split follows identification accuracy rather than the presence of a descriptor slot.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
GUI instruction generation, vision-language models, referring expression generation,
reference-free evaluation, pre-registration
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Given a mobile screenshot and a user goal, a GUI agent predicts an action such as
\texttt{click(872,141)}. The same inputs can instead be turned into a sentence for a person
who needs to be told what to do, for example \emph{tap the pencil icon at the top right of the
note}. Systems serving that consumer are appearing in accessibility and user-support
settings~\cite{guideme}, but the generation problem has had little quantitative treatment. A
recorded coordinate can be checked against a predicted one; a sentence cannot be checked
against a single reference, since many wordings identify the same button and no dataset
enumerates them.

The study is about the model, so it needs a calibrated instrument first. We use
\emph{executability}. The generated sentence goes to a separate grounding
model~\cite{uground} that never sees the gold coordinate, and the step counts as correct
when that model lands within the conventional tolerance of the element the user touched
\emph{and} closer to it than to any other element, and the action class agrees. The term names
a symbolic notion in open grounded planning~\cite{ogp}; ours is behavioural and visual.
Section~\ref{sec:metric} gives the calibration in compressed form, an adequacy check, a
measured ceiling, a measured floor, what the metric weighs, and sensitivity over five hit rules.

We then ask two model questions.

\emph{The first is whether automatically constructed supervision works at all.}
We build the training data from AndroidControl~\cite{androidcontrol} with no new annotation. The scored target is the per-step instruction the dataset already records, and the descriptor slots are derived by rule, not written by another language model. We fine-tune Qwen2.5-VL-3B~\cite{qwen25vl}
with QLoRA~\cite{lora,qlora}. Supervision built this way does work. Executability rises from $47.6\%$ to $59.4\%$, averaged over two seeds on the fine-tuned side. Restricted to the steps on
which the model does not reuse the reference's content words, the gain over the untuned
model is still $+8.4$ points.

\emph{The second is whether it helps to name the element before writing the sentence.}
The referring-expression
tradition says a description is good when a listener recovers the referent from
it~\cite{mao2016,luo2017,yu2017}, and the residual errors of the fine-tuned model are of
that kind. Of the $1{,}825$ failures at seed $101$, $86\%$ carry the correct action with a
wording the grounder does not resolve; some of those steps defeat the references too
(Section~\ref{sec:limits}), and the remainder is an identification problem. So we registered a branch whose target is a structured descriptor (role, name, coordinate, distinguishing cue) followed by the same scored sentence,
byte for byte, as the control.

The registered estimand averages two seeds per branch, and we ran three of the four runs.
At its seed the branch sits $1.93$ points below the control at that same seed, and $2.19$
below the two-seed control mean. The band filed before that branch was trained calls this
inconclusive, while the interval clause of the original registration calls it harm, and
Section~\ref{sec:s2} gives both. The component gains where the descriptor gets both the name
and the point right and loses heavily where it gets both wrong. A second registered stage, a
minimal-pair preference objective aimed at identification itself and chosen after the S2
result, adds $+0.63$ pp at one seed each: inconclusive by the same band, though the same
interval clause would license \emph{positive} here. We do not claim it.

We contribute (i) an instrument for this task calibrated at both ends, with a measured
ceiling of $75.7\%$ and a measured floor, a contentless sentence scoring $12.0\%$ and a
wrong-screen one $6.1\%$ on disjoint intervals; (ii) a supervision pipeline for GUI
instruction generation that adds no annotation of its own, its gain measured at two seeds
and its sign preserved under a second grounder not trained on this corpus; and (iii) a
pre-registered ablation of descriptor-first supervision, run at one of its two planned seeds
and read against the registration rather than around it, with a single-seed diagnosis that
locates the prefix's value in identification accuracy, and a follow-up preference objective
at that layer returning $+0.63$ points over a matched control.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\emph{GUI grounding and agents.} AndroidControl~\cite{androidcontrol} provides
$15{,}283$ human-recorded episodes with per-step instructions and gold touch coordinates.
Grounding models map a text expression to a screen point~\cite{seeclick,osatlas}. We use two
of them as instruments rather than baselines, UGround~\cite{uground}, which is trained on this
corpus, and UI-Venus~\cite{uivenus}, which is not. In AndroidControl the human-written per-step instruction
is an \emph{input} conditioning action prediction, while here it is the generation target and
the only scored output. We claim no novelty for supplying a structured element list, since the AndroidControl authors fine-tune with an accessibility-derived list as input, Mind2Web~\cite{mind2web} ranks noisy candidates before generation, and Widget Captioning~\cite{widgetcap} conditions on the view hierarchy.

\emph{Discriminative description.} Producing a description that identifies one object
among distractors is referring-expression generation, and its discriminative objective has
been comprehension-in-the-loop from the start, from maximum mutual information~\cite{mao2016}
to explicit listener rewards~\cite{luo2017,yu2017}. The closest supervised precedent is
Widget Captioning~\cite{widgetcap}, which identifies the problem of visually identical
widgets but trains with plain cross-entropy. What we add is narrower than that tradition. We
place discriminativeness in the \emph{training target} for GUI instructions and then measure
whether it pays. Outside the GUI domain, making a model emit grounding coordinates before it
answers helps when \emph{trained} rather than prompted~\cite{gcot}. That preprint is the
nearest precedent for our branch, and our result should be read against it. Nearer home,
removing the inner monologue from the Aguvis training targets costs $11.4$ points on
AndroidControl-Low, our subtraction from their ablation table~\cite{aguvis}. That target is consumed by an action head, never handed to a person, so it does not settle the question for our setting.

\emph{Anchoring a metric outside the generator.} Verifying generated text against an external
source instead of the generator's own beliefs is the principle behind ALOHa~\cite{aloha}, which
uses an object detector alongside the references, and perturbation checklists are an
established way of validating a metric~\cite{sai2021}. Closest to our setting is
navigation-instruction evaluation, where a reference-free model correlates best per sentence
while its authors still recommend a reference-based score for ranking systems~\cite{zhao2021},
a split we respect by claiming only at system level. Neither obvious anchor is safe here.
Untrained panels separate machine from human text at chance~\cite{clark2021}, and grounder
accuracy measured on one ``best'' instruction per element overstates what these models can
do~\cite{groundersunderstand}.
The term \emph{executability} is used differently in open grounded planning~\cite{ogp}, as
the share of plans whose every action appears in a given action library. That is a symbolic
lookup over text, while ours is behavioural and visual.

\section{Task, Supervision, and Branches}
\label{sec:data}

From a screenshot $I$ and a goal $q$ we produce one instruction $s$ naming the element to touch and the action to perform, addressed to a human reader.

\emph{Supervision.} No public redistribution of AndroidControl carries the instruction, the
screenshot and the accessibility tree together, so we join two mirrors on
\texttt{(episode\_id, step\_id)} and take trees for $99{,}131$ screens from the same release. An off-by-one join would corrupt every label while leaving the pipeline functional, so we test the alignment. OCR text at the gold touch point appears in the reference on $48\%$ of $n=400$ sampled steps, against $20\%$ under a shifted control. The corpus holds $64{,}567$ training and $6{,}958$ test steps over $12{,}895$ and $1{,}432$
tasks; $4{,}463$ of the test steps are touch steps and form the scoring population used throughout. The split is held out by task with zero overlap, verified at full scale. On $120$ of the $99{,}131$ screens, sampled at random, only
$12.6\%$ of elements carry any name, a shortfall reported elsewhere too~\cite{chen2020}, so we pass every screenshot through OCR, used both as an input feature and as a name source. Each descriptor label is built by rule from the smallest accessibility box containing the gold
point: role from the tree, name from the tree when it passes a validity check and from OCR
inside the box otherwise, the point on a $[0,1000]$ grid, and a distinguishing cue, the nearest
OCR string outside the box with its relative direction.
Across the $41{,}099$ training descriptor labels, $73.6\%$ of labels carry a clear name, $4.4\%$ only a symbol and $22.0\%$
none, while $7.6\%$ of target elements share their name with another element on the same
screen. The construction, its two filters and its label-quality audit are reported in full in
a companion paper~\cite{companion}, whose output we use here.

\emph{Branches.} Four datasets share identical inputs and differ only in the generation
target: \textbf{S1}, the sentence alone; \textbf{S2}, descriptor then sentence; \textbf{S2r},
a false descriptor from a different screen with randomised coordinates, token-length matched;
and \textbf{S2-nopoint}, S2 without the coordinate slot. An S2 target reads
\texttt{<desc>tappable text | Search here | <point>440,91</point> | just above the text
"Coffee"</desc>} followed by the same scored sentence as S1. Nine structural invariants are checked after every rebuild and all nine hold at full scale.
The one that matters here is that the scored sentence is byte-identical across branches on all
$64{,}567$ examples, so a difference between branches is a difference in what precedes it. We registered S2r and S2-nopoint as attribution controls but did not run them, for the
same reason as the second treatment seed (Section~\ref{sec:design}).

\section{Descriptor-First Supervision}
\label{sec:method}

The intervention is a change of target, not of architecture or input; S2's only added cost is
the descriptor tokens it decodes before each sentence. S1 and S2 read the same prompt, made of the goal, the reference instructions of the previous three steps and the OCR strings, and both are scored on the sentence alone, with any
descriptor stripped before scoring. S2 differs only in that the target begins with the four
descriptor slots of Section~\ref{sec:data}, the sentence following unchanged.

The coordinate slot is included because it is a clean label at zero construction cost, and
because prior ablations show that intermediate steps carrying an explicit point help, while
free-form verbal chains do not~\cite{aguvis,gcot}.
The descriptor is emitted rather than consumed, so the model must produce its own
identification before writing, which makes the branch an ablation of \emph{discriminative
commitment} and not of extra input. The consequence is that a wrong name and point are
inherited by the sentence that follows them. Section~\ref{sec:diag} measures how often that
happens and what it costs.

\section{The Instrument}
\label{sec:metric}

\subsection{Definition}
Let $s$ be the generated sentence with any descriptor removed, $I$ the screenshot, $g$
the gold touch coordinate, and $E$ the set of accessibility elements marked visible to the
user and at least $8$ px on each side, after merging those whose centres lie within
$24$\,dp ($63$ px at the modal density) of one another or of $g$. Let $G$ be a separate
grounding model, not the model under test and without access to $g$, returning $p = G(I,s)$.
The step is correct when all three hold: \emph{(i) action}, the canonicalised action
types of $s$ and of the reference agree, twelve surface verbs mapping to a single touch
class, with type, scroll and long-press kept distinct from touch, while \emph{back} collapses
into touch whenever the sentence also carries a motion verb, which
Section~\ref{sec:alt} quantifies; \emph{(ii) polarity},
$s$ and the reference do not name opposite states of the same control, checked against
nine toggle pairs; \emph{(iii) localisation}, $p$ lies within the conventional $14\%$ tolerance of~\cite{aitw}, which we implement per axis as $|p_x - g_x| \le 0.14W$, $|p_y - g_y| \le 0.14H$ ($\pm151$ px horizontally against $\pm336$ px vertically on a
$1080\times2400$ screen, a rectangle and not a disc) and without their same-bounding-box
disjunct, \emph{and} no competing element centre is closer to $p$ than $g$ itself. The point
that defines the cell is the recorded touch coordinate, not the centre of the box containing it. Condition (iii) refines the tolerance
test and never accepts a step that test rejects, so the two are not alternatives.
Executability is the proportion of touch steps satisfying all three.

The rubric is a recorded human touch coordinate, so this is not LLM-as-judge: $G$ supplies only a location, the comparison against $g$ supplies the verdict, and the same $G$ scores every branch.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\columnwidth]{fig_voronoi.png}
\caption{A step on which the tolerance test alone cannot carry the judgement. The user touched
the \emph{Birthday} row, and the fine-tuned model's sentence sends the grounder inside that
row's cell. The untuned model wrote ``Click on the `Gender' section\ldots'', and the grounder
followed it to the row above, $128$ px away but inside the $\pm336$ px vertical tolerance,
which the conventional rule scores correct and the cell rule does not.}
\label{fig:cell}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:cell} shows a step on which the two rules disagree. With the grounder's answer
displaced to the centre of the nearest competing element, the tolerance test still awards
credit on $843$ of the $1{,}000$ injected draws ($250$ steps with four injected angles each),
while the cell rule awards $2.8\%$ and still holds a ceiling of $99.7\%$ under a $3\%$
injected offset, $97$ points of range against the tolerance test's $16$. We report the tolerance
score alongside the headline throughout, never as the headline.

\subsection{Calibration}
Five measurements support the readings in this paper, the first three fixing the scale and the
last two testing what it responds to.

\emph{Adequacy.} A pass condition of median localisation error at most $3\%$ of screen
width was fixed before any compute was spent, derived from screen geometry. The tolerance
margin, half the distance from the element to be touched to the nearest other element after
merging, has median $6.2\%$ of width and 10th percentile $3.6\%$. UGround-V1-2B attains
$0.7\%$ on $300$ reference-branch steps, the only branch this check certifies; on the branches
under test the same median is $2.00\%$ and $7.82\%$. The condition is met there, but the error
distribution is bimodal, the grounder landing either close to the element's centre or nowhere
near it, so a percentile condition would have been the better choice, and one declared check
for a broken installation fired. On $30.7\%$ of steps the horizontal answer is exactly screen
centre, partly a property of the data and partly the grounder abandoning the axis. Both causes
lower every branch, the references included.

\emph{Ceiling.} Passing every human-written reference through the same pipeline scores
$\mathbf{75.7\%}$ $[74.1, 77.3]$ over all $4{,}463$ touch steps. Every score below is read
against $75.7$, not against $100$. An earlier $300$-step estimate of $70.0\%$ was nearly six points low and is withdrawn as a ceiling.

\emph{Floor.} Replacing every sentence with the contentless \emph{tap the button} scores
$12.0\%$ $[9.7, 14.4]$ on a random $800$-step slice whose ceiling is $74.9\%$, so the
usable range is $62.9$ points and the metric is not carried by what the grounder can guess
from the screenshot alone. A genuine reference sentence written for a \emph{different} step,
with correct register, matched length and wrong content, scores lower still, $6.1\%$
$[4.5, 7.9]$, on disjoint intervals: a wrong sentence sends the grounder somewhere definite
and wrong, while an empty one leaves it to a weak prior. That control holds annotator style
fixed, so this also rules out the reading that the metric rewards register.

\emph{What the metric weighs.} On the same slice, keeping the locative clause and replacing
the element name with the generic \emph{the item} costs $28.5$ points on the $193$ steps it
touches ($b{=}3$, $c{=}58$, $p<0.001$), against $3.5$ points for deleting the locative clause
and keeping the name on a near-identical population of $198$ ($b{=}1$, $c{=}8$, $p{=}0.046$).
Naming the element is worth roughly eight times saying where it is. Meaning-preserving
rewrites, verb substitution and fronting the locative clause, move executability by $+0.35$ pp
($[-0.59, +1.29]$) over the $1{,}139$ steps they alter, so the instrument is insensitive to
these rewrites while sensitive to content.

\subsection{Robustness of the instrument}
We measured how much the result depends on the hit rule.
Rescoring three branches on $698$ steps under five rules (Euclidean disc, per-axis
rectangle, the cell rule as implemented, a cell rule seeded at the box centre, and a
nearest-box rule) gives ceilings of $80.2$, $82.2$, $74.2$, $56.6$ and $82.2$, and a
fine-tuning gain of $+12.6$, $+13.0$, $+11.7$, $+9.5$ and $+13.0$ points (the implemented
rule reads $74.2$ on this slice against $75.7$ on the full population). The absolute level
moves by up to $26$ points, the ordering never does, and the gain stays between $9.5$ and
$13.0$. The instrument is also deterministic. On the $2{,}811$ steps where two training runs
emitted byte-identical sentences, the grounder returned the same coordinates and the same
verdict on all $2{,}811$.

Intervals are nonparametric cluster bootstraps with $10{,}000$ resamples, clustered by
application where one can be attributed and by task otherwise ($G = 1{,}091$, of which $259$
are application clusters and $832$ single-task clusters covering $59\%$ of steps;
$G_{\text{eff}} = 454.3$ under Kish's formula~\cite{kish1965}). The clustering rule was fixed
in advance because it determines the available power~\cite{cameron2008}. The design effect is
$1.10$ and standard errors are contrast-specific, $0.785$ pp for $\mathrm{S1} - \mathrm{Base}$,
giving a minimum detectable paired effect of $2.2$ points at $80\%$ power. That covers
instrument noise alone; a contrast across two \emph{training runs} also carries the seed term
of Section~\ref{sec:design}.

\section{Experimental Design and Pre-Registration}
\label{sec:design}

All branches fine-tune Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct~\cite{qwen25vl} with QLoRA~\cite{lora,qlora}
under LlamaFactory~\cite{llamafactory}: 4-bit NF4, LoRA rank $8$, $\alpha = 16$, dropout
$0.05$, adapters on \texttt{q,k,v,o,gate,up,down}, vision tower and projector frozen, context
$2560$, effective batch $16$, learning rate $1\times10^{-4}$, cosine schedule, two epochs, on a
single A100 ($8{,}072$ steps, $\approx 23$ h). One configuration file serves every branch, four
keys changing. The trainable parameter count is $14{,}966{,}784$, confirming the vision tower
stayed frozen, and losses on an L4 and an A100 agree to three digits with identical
\texttt{total\_flos}.

Inference is greedy, one sentence per step, the descriptor stripped by a fixed pattern so no branch is scored on text the others cannot produce. The prompt template is byte-identical across branches and between training and inference, the untuned model included. Because the history carries human-written sentences rather than an action log, scoring is teacher-forced on context, which conditions absolute scores; branch differences are read under the same history for every branch.

The design was committed to version control on 5 August 2026, before any training run, and
amendments are appended with dates and never edited in place. It states the four outcomes as
interval conditions: a $\Delta$ whose interval lies wholly above zero is positive when its
magnitude clears the seed term and a trend when it does not; one whose interval covers zero
with $|\Delta|$ below the detectable effect is inconclusive; one whose interval lies wholly
below zero is harm. The registered quantity is
$\Delta = \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S2}) - \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S1})$ over \emph{two seeds
per branch}. A dated amendment of 17 August, filed after the control branch was scored and
before the treatment branch was trained, restates the same four outcomes as a band on the
magnitude of $\Delta$: the inconclusive row spans $-2.8 \ldots +1.7$ pp, and only the
positive and negative rows keep an interval condition. The negative edge is the doubled
threshold and the positive edge the two-seed one, so the band is wider against harm than
against benefit. The operative threshold of $2.8$ pp combines the bootstrap standard error of
the seed pair, $0.38$ pp, with a seed term of $0.46$ pp, doubled because it rests on one
degree of freedom; both are variance estimates read off the already-scored control pair and
neither comes from a treatment score.
Section~\ref{sec:mindesc} reads the stage-2 pair $\mathrm{MIN} - \mathrm{CE2}$ against the
undoubled counterpart of $2.11$ pp, which fits a design carrying \emph{one} seed per branch
and lets the seed term enter as $\sqrt{2}\,\sigma$. The registered two-seed figure is
$1.67$ pp. The band and the interval clause disagree twice, calling the treatment branch harm
and the stage-2 pair positive where the band reads inconclusive in both;
Sections~\ref{sec:s2} and~\ref{sec:mindesc} take the band in each, once in our favour and once
against it. Twenty-seven amendments are recorded, twenty-one before any executability score
existed. The six later ones do not all run one way. Most raised the headroom or lowered a
threshold, but the amendment of 17 August replaced a clause under which any negative upper
bound was harm with a band requiring $-2.8$ pp, which makes harm \emph{harder} to declare
here; and the first of the six cut the control branch's share of the withdrawn $70.0$
estimate, $84.4\%$, to $78.0\%$ of $75.7$.

We ran three of the four registered runs. On 23 August 2026 we chose, on budget grounds, not
to train the second treatment seed, and that choice is final. Every statement we make about
that branch is single-seed and exploratory, and no substitute for the registered test.

