\subsection{Automatic supervision works and replicates}
Fine-tuning on supervision assembled without new annotation moves the model from $47.6$ to $59.4$ points, closing $42\%$ of the distance
to the human-reference level. Paired over the same steps (McNemar), S1 over Base is
$+11.5$ points at seed 101 ($b = 284$, $c = 798$, $\chi^2 = 243.2$) and $+12.0$ at seed
202, both $p<0.001$, where $b$ counts steps the lower-scoring branch resolves and the
higher-scoring one does not.

All rates use the same denominator of $4{,}463$ touch steps, and the three empty sentences are scored zero, since refusing to answer is what the metric must penalise.

The $59.4$ is inflated by the quarter of steps on which S1 reproduces the reference's
content words. Restricted to the $3{,}367$ steps where the model writes its own sentence,
Base, S1 and the human references read $44.7$, $53.1$ and $75.0$, the fraction closed is $28\%$, and the gap is
still $+8.4$ points ($b=264$, $c=547$, $p<0.001$). Further readings, all post-hoc, leave the
ordering intact. Dropping the action-class condition and scoring localisation alone leaves
$+11.3$ points, and the untuned model names the correct action class \emph{more} often
($96.5\%$ against $94.4\%$). Across the $1{,}091$ clusters, S1 is higher on $395$, level on
$565$ and lower on $131$, with the same ordering at seed $202$. Application overlap brings no
advantage either, S1 scoring $59.1\%$ on applications seen in training ($n{=}1{,}737$),
$59.0\%$ on unseen ones ($n{=}78$) and $59.1\%$ where none can be attributed
($n{=}2{,}648$).

\emph{Retraining noise.} A gap between systems must be read against the movement from rebuilding one. Retraining S1 under a second seed shifts the score by $+0.52$ points, interval $[-0.2, +1.3]$, McNemar $p = 0.19$. The sharper contrast is in the verdict counts. The seeds disagree on $6.4\%$
of steps, against the $24.2\%$ that fine-tuning moves in either direction ($b+c$ above).
Step-level agreement across seeds is $93.6\%$ ($\kappa = 0.867$), but $63\%$ of all pairs are
byte-identical sentences a deterministic instrument must agree on. On the $1{,}652$ steps where the runs wrote something different it is $82.6\%$
($\kappa = 0.650$), the figure we quote.

\subsection{A second grounder}
\label{sec:venus}
Every number above is conditioned on one grounder, trained on $47$K elements from the
AndroidControl training split and so familiar with the register S1 is trained to imitate.
We re-scored three branches on a $2{,}532$-step slice, the largest a week of free GPU quota
allows, with UI-Venus-Ground-7B~\cite{uivenus}, not trained on AndroidControl, at a
$1{,}272$-token image budget chosen for speed once the two adequacy intervals overlapped
(Table~\ref{tab:venus}).

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Two grounders on the same $2{,}532$-step slice, except the last row. The positive
control $\mathrm{S1}-\mathrm{Base}$ is raw on the slice, while the $\mathrm{S2}-\mathrm{S1}$
row is rescaled to the $4{,}463$-step denominator used elsewhere. The full-scale reference of
$75.7$ lies above the last row's interval, since $300$ steps were too few to place the
reference, which is why that estimate was withdrawn as a ceiling
(Section~\ref{sec:metric}).}
\label{tab:venus}
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Contrast & UGround & UI-Venus \\
\midrule
S1 $-$ Base (pos.\ control) & $+10.35\,[+8.39, +12.40]$ & $+9.68\,[+7.60, +11.78]$ \\
S2 $-$ S1 (single seed) & $-1.93\,[-3.08, -0.78]$   & $-1.21\,[-2.30, -0.12]$ \\
\midrule
Human refs.\ ($n{=}300$) & $70.0\,[64.5, 75.3]$      & $69.3\,[63.8, 74.8]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The positive control keeps $94\%$ of its magnitude and the two adequacy ceilings are within
$0.7$ points, so the explanation that fine-tuning merely teaches the annotator's register,
a register the first grounder was trained on, does not survive. The sign of the S2 contrast
holds too, though the two scorings share checkpoints and so do not substitute for the
missing seed. A paired difference-in-differences puts that contrast $+1.26$ pp less negative under UI-Venus, $[+0.04, +2.51]$, weak and exploratory evidence that cannot carry the reading that the first grounder is what makes S2 lose, which would require the contrast to reach zero. Part of the shrinkage is mechanical, since UI-Venus scores $2.8$--$4.1$ points lower on every branch.

\subsection{The descriptor branch}
\label{sec:s2}
The registered comparison is $\mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S2}) - \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S1})$
over two seeds each. We ran one of the two treatment seeds, so only a narrower statement is available.
\emph{With Qwen2.5-VL-3B under QLoRA and the present automatic labelling pipeline, the
S2/101 checkpoint scores $1.93$ points below S1/101 ($b = 254$, $c = 340$, interval
$[-3.06, -0.75]$ on the full population, against the $[-3.08, -0.78]$ Table~\ref{tab:venus}
obtains by rescaling from the slice) and $2.44$ points below S1/202 over the full $4{,}463$ steps, the sign
holds under UI-Venus, and the registered estimand remains incomplete.} The two clauses of
the registration disagree here, so we give both. Against the band of 17 August, $-2.19$ pp
relative to the two-seed control mean falls inside $-2.8 \ldots +1.7$ and reads as
\emph{inconclusive}; the missing run means it stays there. Against the interval clause of
the original document the paired interval above has a negative upper bound, which that
clause calls \emph{harm}. The band is the later and more specific of the two and it was filed
before this branch was trained, so we take it as operative, a tie-break that on this branch
runs in our favour. The magnitude also sits inside the $2.8$ pp band and outside the $2.11$
and $1.67$ pp thresholds of Section~\ref{sec:design}. The $2.2$ pp figure of Section~\ref{sec:metric} covers instrument noise only and is not the threshold here.

What the missing run would have had to produce is fixed by the registered thresholds. To bring the two-seed mean to the weakly-positive edge of $\Delta = +1.7$ pp, S2/202 would have had to score $65.0\%$, roughly five points above the best checkpoint we have measured, MIN at $60.0$; for the negative band it would have had to score at or below $56.0\%$.

Two things do not depend on the missing seed. The branch is far above the untuned model, $+9.6$ points $[+8.2, +11.1]$, so the descriptor target does not break fine-tuning. It also emits its coordinate slot well, with $71.0\%$ of the points it generates on steps with a parsable descriptor landing inside the $14\%$ window at a median horizontal error of $0.81\%$ of screen width, so the descriptor target does not cost the ability to point.

\subsection{A preference objective at the descriptor layer}
\label{sec:mindesc}
Section~\ref{sec:diag} points at identification accuracy, not at the prefix, so we
ran the objective registered for it, MIN. From the S2 checkpoint it applies $800$ further updates on minimal pairs whose accepted side is the gold descriptor followed by the
sentence and whose rejected side is a wrong descriptor followed by the \emph{same sentence,
verbatim}, so the preference term can be reduced only by choosing the right element. Its
attribution control, CE2, is plain supervised training on the accepted side alone, with the
same checkpoint, the same $800$ updates, the same schedule and no preference term.

Both branches are in Table~\ref{tab:main}. The registered primary averages this pair over two
seeds and we have one seed of it. At that seed the paired difference is $+0.63$ pp,
$[+0.16, +1.10]$, with $b = 42$, $c = 70$, $\chi^2 = 6.5$ and $p = 0.011$. That interval carries instrument noise only, its own cluster-bootstrap standard error being $0.24$ pp. The minimum detectable effect for one seed per branch, $2.11$ pp by Section~\ref{sec:design}, is above it, and $+0.63$ is $1.4\times$ the seed term. By the band, fixed before this branch was trained, this is
\emph{inconclusive}. The divergence of Section~\ref{sec:s2} applies here too, and in this
direction it runs against us: the interval excludes zero, so the interval clause would
license \emph{positive}. We do not claim it.

The preference branch is $+2.87$ pp above
S2 $[+2.03, +3.76]$, but $+2.24$ $[+1.41, +3.11]$ of that is attributable to the control ($800$ further supervised updates, no preference term anywhere), and only $+0.63$ sits on top of it.
In this pair roughly four fifths of the movement belongs to the control branch, $+2.24$ of $+2.87$, so the total is not the effect of the objective. A steeper split appears one layer down, at the descriptor, where the control lifts joint name-and-point accuracy by
$+5.9$ pp against $+0.8$ pp for the preference term, a split of $88/12$, measured on the $3{,}473$ steps carrying a gold name without calling the grounder. The stage does not overtake plain sentence supervision either, since MIN $-$ S1 is $+0.94$ $[-0.09, +2.05]$, $p = 0.11$, and CE2 $-$ S1 is $+0.31$, $p = 0.61$.



\emph{A registered variant stopped by its feasibility check.} Two sources for the rejected side
were registered: the heuristic one used here, the nearest same-role element $80$--$350$ px
away, and an on-policy one taking the element the checkpoint itself names when it names the
wrong one. The second was stopped before any training, $14{,}000$ screens giving $459$ usable
pairs, $3.3\%$ against a threshold of $25\%$ and $14.1\%$ on the most generous denominator we
can defend. The reason is measurable: over the $3{,}246$ wrong-name steps, the distance from
the element named to the element to be touched has quartiles of $70$, $351$ and $748$ px, so
the window rejects $76.5\%$ of them. The errors are the right element under another name or a
different region entirely, neither of them the local confusion an on-policy negative presumes.
The surviving source therefore trains on a neighbour confusion the data rarely contains, which
is a candidate reason why $+0.63$ is as small as it is.

\section{Conditions Under Which the Descriptor Pays}
\label{sec:diag}

This section is exploratory and rests on one treatment seed. The mechanism it exposes is what a follow-up objective must act on, and two of its components, the activation slice of Section~\ref{sec:noslice} and the strict canonicalisation of Section~\ref{sec:alt}, were registered before any treatment score existed.

\subsection{The component works when identification succeeds}
The branch emits its own descriptor, so its identification can be scored against the gold
labels independently of the sentence. Table~\ref{tab:cross} crosses name against coordinate
correctness on the $3{,}245$ steps that carry a gold name and receive a parsable descriptor. A
further $228$ steps carry a gold name but emit no descriptor, the gold-name subset of the
$325$ of Section~\ref{sec:noslice}, and $881$ carry a descriptor but no gold name to check it
against, so the table cannot include either group. The remaining $109$ steps carry neither,
and all of them enter the totals. There the branch is $+1.48$ pp
\emph{above} its control, interval $[-1.16, +4.10]$, which covers zero.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Executability by whether the branch's own descriptor got the name and the
coordinate right. Entries are executability in \%, $\Delta = \mathrm{S2} - \mathrm{S1}$ in
points computed before rounding, and Ceil.\ the human references on the same steps; one
treatment seed. Base and Ceil.\ are not conditioned on the treatment, so they indicate how
hard each row is. The stratification is post-hoc and is not a causal claim. Cluster-bootstrap
intervals on $\Delta$ clear zero in the first and last rows only, $[+4.5, +7.0]$ and
$[-28.5, -21.9]$.}
\label{tab:cross}
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrr}
\toprule
Name & Point & $n$ & Base & S1 & S2 & $\Delta$ & Ceil. \\
\midrule
correct & inside  & $1{,}871$ & 69.0 & 81.4 & \textbf{87.1} & $\mathbf{+5.7}$ & 86.6 \\
correct & outside & $184$     & 23.4 & 25.5 & 26.1 & $+0.5$ & 31.0 \\
wrong   & inside  & $452$     & 41.4 & 56.4 & 57.5 & $+1.1$ & 72.8 \\
wrong   & outside & $738$     & 24.4 & 30.8 & \textbf{5.6} & $\mathbf{-25.2}$ & 66.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The branch clears the control in the first row and falls behind across its complement, by
$-13.1$ pp on the remaining $1{,}374$ steps, interval $[-15.4, -10.8]$; the two middle rows
are indistinguishable and the whole of that deficit sits in the fourth. With both the name
wrong and the coordinate outside tolerance, on $738$ steps or $16.5\%$ of the $4{,}463$,
executability falls to $5.6\%$. The cost here comes from commitment: having named the wrong element, the branch writes a sentence faithful to it, while the control branch, which names no element, still retrieves the right one on $30.8\%$ of them.

The objection is that this stratification conditions on a variable the treatment itself
produces, so the failing row may simply be the hard steps. The Base and Ceil.\ columns test
that; neither is conditioned on the treatment. If the row were merely hard, the references
would fall with it, yet they reach $66.3\%$ there, $9.4$ points below their $75.7$, while the treatment reaches $5.6$ and the untuned model $24.4$. The row is harder than average and that does not account for a fall of that size. A clean test would need a difficulty measure fixed before the treatment ran, so the confound is narrowed without being removed. One row does behave as the objection predicts.
The row \emph{name correct, point outside}, with a ceiling of $31.0\%$, is a region the
instrument largely cannot see, and the near-zero $\Delta$ there is an absence of signal and not evidence of an absent effect.

The decomposition of the total is arithmetic. The grouping carries the same caveat, but the
two parts sum to the whole whatever it means: the $92.7\%$ of steps carrying a descriptor
contribute $-1.28$ pp and the $7.3\%$ that do not contribute $-0.65$ pp.

\subsection{A third of the difference sits in \texorpdfstring{$7.3\%$}{7.3\%} of steps}
\label{sec:noslice}
The slice separating steps where the branch emits a descriptor from those where it does not was registered on 19 August, before any S2 score existed.

On the $4{,}138$ steps where a descriptor is emitted, S1 and S2 read $62.2$ and $60.8$
($\Delta = -1.38$, $[-2.54, -0.19]$); on the $325$ where none is, they read $19.7$ and $10.8$
($\Delta = -8.92$, $[-12.57, -5.25]$), one treatment seed throughout.
The $7.3\%$ of steps where the branch emits nothing carry $34\%$ of the total difference, and there the model misjudges the \emph{action class}, the reference saying \emph{Click on the close icon} where the branch writes \emph{Swipe up}. References score $68.3\%$ there, so the steps are solvable and the instrument is not blind. The descriptor is not the origin of the failure, since the untuned model names the correct action class on $83.4\%$ of these steps against $55.1\%$ for the control, which carries no descriptor at all; the treatment's $38.8\%$ is consistent with fine-tuning damaging the action prior and the descriptor target deepening the damage.
One limitation attaches to this slice. The $325$ steps are defined by the treatment's own
behaviour, so ``it does badly there'' is partly definitional. What is not definitional is that the control also scores only $19.7\%$.

\subsection{Four alternative explanations tested and set aside}
\label{sec:alt}
\emph{Length.} The difference is negative on long sentences ($-1.85$) and on short ones
($-2.00$), and the two branches' length distributions all but coincide (median $33$ and
$34$ characters). \emph{Application slice.} It is negative on seen ($-2.25$), unseen
($-6.41$, $n = 78$ and uninformative) and unattributable ($-1.59$) steps alike. \emph{Canonicalisation.} The \emph{go back} $\to$ touch collapse in the action check,
condition (i) of Section~\ref{sec:metric}, hides exactly this failure mode, $65$ treatment sentences containing
\emph{go back} passing the action check while only $21$ are executable, so we re-ran with
the strict variant registered on 16 August before any treatment score. The difference moves
from $-1.93$ to $-1.86$, all four branches dropping $0.20$--$0.31$ points with the ordering
unchanged. \emph{Label noise.} On the $26$ steps whose descriptor label is OCR
garbage the difference is $+7.69$ ($p = 0.63$), the opposite direction.

\subsection{Relation to the grounding-first prior}
The reported gains for targets that ground before answering~\cite{gcot} were not measured on
this task, and three differences matter. Their supervision is a question--answer pair whose
grounded object is what the question is \emph{about}, so a grounding error shows in the
answer, while ours admits a fluent wrong sentence after a wrong descriptor. Their grounding
target is checked against annotation during training, ours at inference against the model's
own identification. Table~\ref{tab:cross} measures what that costs. And their gains are read on
answer accuracy, whose floor is near chance, while executability runs from a measured $12.0$
to a measured $75.7$; the two scales do not convert, so their gains and ours cannot be
compared in size. Our result neither confirms nor contradicts that prior. It is one
exploratory, single-seed observation in which the prefix gains only where the branch's own
identification was correct, on a split conditioned post-hoc on the treatment's output, and
where identification is hard, $22.0\%$ of touch targets carrying no name in tree or
OCR.

\subsection{Implications for the intervention}
The supervision is not uniformly inert, since it adds $5.7$ points on the $1{,}871$ steps where the descriptor gets the name and the point right and subtracts $13.1$ on the other $1{,}374$. The presence of a slot does not explain that split. The two middle rows, $+1.1$ $[-2.5, +4.7]$ where only the point is right and $+0.5$ $[-2.8, +4.3]$ where only the name is, separate neither from zero nor from each other, so neither slot can be given a verdict. The rows are defined by identification correctness, so this describes the split rather than explaining it; what it does rule out is that either slot alone accounts for it. Lifting the fourth row, where both name and point are wrong, to the control's level on its $738$ steps, membership held fixed, is worth $4.2$ points arithmetically over the full $4{,}463$-step population rather than the $3{,}245$ crossed here. Section~\ref{sec:mindesc} reports the objective
registered for exactly that, and what it returned. Choosing this objective \emph{after} seeing the S2 result is post-hoc and recorded as such.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

\emph{The registered estimand is incomplete.} The treatment branch has one seed, and so do both stage-2 branches, so their contrasts are exploratory even where the slice was registered. Three readings are not available from these numbers: that the hypothesis was refuted, or that the treatment is harmful by the registered \emph{two-seed} quantity, whose second seed does not exist, or that a McNemar $p$ settles a question in which seed variance is a component.

\emph{Ceiling and blind region.} The human-reference level is $75.7\%$. The references fail on
$1{,}083$ steps, $72\%$ of those because the grounder lands more than $14\%$ of screen width
away, and $935$ steps defeat Base, S1 and the references alike. The instrument catches a sentence that
names the wrong element, but not a point that lands slightly off inside the right one, so it
is blind below the radius at which neighbouring elements are merged.

\emph{Grounder dependence.} Our headline grounder is trained on the AndroidControl split, and
both grounders are built on Qwen-family backbones, so neither a register effect nor a
same-family effect is excluded, and no human evaluation anchors either. Our rewrites keep the element name verbatim, so we probed phrasing, not the referring expression that~\cite{groundersunderstand} perturbs. Their $84\%$ is the yield of
an adversarial search over Windows desktops rather than a paraphrase failure rate, and the
mobile case they leave open is ours.

\emph{Split composition.} The split is held out by task, not by application. Of the
attributable applications $95.6\%$ also occur in training, and $59\%$ of scored steps cannot be attributed at all, \emph{unknown} and never read as \emph{unseen}. Because that overlap would favour us, we report no standalone comparison against an external
model. Nor is task disjointness the same as phrase disjointness, since $17.3\%$ of test references recur verbatim in training, concentrated in a training subset of $2.6\%$ of steps.

\emph{Scope of the construct.} Every claim is at the level of a system, never of a single
sentence, and the one setting where this family of measures has been validated against
people recommends a reference-based score for ranking systems even while its reference-free
model correlates best per instruction~\cite{zhao2021}. The metric asks whether a grounding
model reaches the right element, not whether a person would, so a system could climb it by writing for the grounder. We bound that without excluding it, since the floor controls of
Section~\ref{sec:metric} show a fluent sentence with wrong content scoring below the
contentless one.

\emph{External validity.} Every result here is confined to a single screen, English
interfaces, one dataset, one 3B base model and one training configuration. Scoring is also
teacher-forced on human-written history, so absolute levels do not transfer to a system
running its own trajectory.

\section{Conclusion}

We built supervision for GUI instruction generation without collecting new annotation and measured what it achieves on a scale calibrated at both ends. Executability moves from $47.6$ to $59.4$ points at two seeds, against a human-reference score of $75.7$ and a measured floor of $12.0$, and the sign is preserved under a second grounder not trained on AndroidControl.

Whether naming the element first adds anything was to be answered over two seeds per branch; we ran three of the four and stopped there, and the two registered attribution controls were cancelled with that run. At its single seed the treatment sits $2.19$ below the two-seed control mean. The band filed before that branch was trained calls this inconclusive, while the interval clause of the same registration calls it harm, and Section~\ref{sec:s2} gives both.

The split by the branch's own descriptor is exploratory and rests on one seed. The prefix gains $+5.7$ points on the $1{,}871$ steps where it gets the name and the point right and loses $13.1$ on the other $1{,}374$, the loss concentrated on the $738$ where both are wrong. A separate failure of the action prior, which fine-tuning introduces before any descriptor exists, carries $34\%$ of the total difference on $7.3\%$ of steps. A further $881$ steps, which carry no gold name and cannot enter that table, run the other way at $+1.48$ pp with an interval covering zero. The preference stage adds $+0.63$ pp over a matched control, below the $2.11$ pp detectable at one seed, and most of the stage-2 rise belongs to that control. What has to move is identification accuracy.

